text:
Oh you who lie beneath the earth, oh sorrowful people
bloodied by love's blow,
and you who burn continually
for loving well, do not forget me;
weep, showing your burning heart,
pull out your hair by handfuls;
show the piteous heart wounded
by the golden arrow, and hurt.


With his arrows Love wounds and has wounded us,
each to a different degree;
according to the blow, so it will be felt,
and so the pain will also be wept.
Of lead, silver, and finest gold
are the arrows of which I have spoken;
each one causes its affliction,
according to the difference between one and another.


Love spent the golden arrows it had
on lovers of the past;
it left one forgotten, and stubbornly
with it caused me a hundred thousand pains;
if many had been killed by this blow,
there is now no mortal blow in love,
for it wounds only with the silver ones,
so that it kills no wounded man.


It uses the lead ones and wounds now,
but this wound never draws blood;
seeing himself so weak at that hour,
he broke his bow and took flight;
he asks for peace, shouting hour after hour,
everyone walks safely and forgets him;
from today there is no need to fear his war,
for Love's power lies beneath the earth.


I alone remain dead and badly wounded:
his peace was a fierce battle for me;
if it had been war that overcame me,
I would have been defeated in peace, and a slave;
the world at peace and I thrust into war,
because Love has brought his war to an end;
see what remedy I hope for this wound,
when she for whom I die feels no pain.
